nested/wrapG1-first-loss.md.text:
# A real nested-call regression from MAY-to-STAR initial conversion

Status: measured native case; read-only decode, 7 November 2026. No tested
source, output, policy or repository file is changed by this decoder.

`wrapG1` has one exact T source, four methods, ordinary calls and one exact
T sink. All objects and the Element slot are initialized finite-IR
preconditions. There is no loop, cleaner, ND rule, static or injected guidance.

```java
void main() {
    Object s = sourceExactT();
    temp.g = s;
    next.f = temp;
    Object x = getF(next);
    Object y = wrapG(x);
    out.Element.h = y;
    sinkExactT(out.Element.h);
}
Object getF(Object p) { return p.f; }
Object wrapG(Object p) { Object v = getG(p); return v; }
Object getG(Object p) { return p.g; }
```

Both variants use FULL marks and limits `1,1,2,...`. The current variant
reports once in run-3. The all-MAY conversion reports zero times. Both reach
and complete run-3 and stop with `NO_DEMAND_EDGE`; this is not a timeout.
The expected-one-report test is GREEN for current and RED for all-MAY.
All case observations are present: 135 current and 79 all-MAY rows.

`F` below is the abstract FLOW mark; `T` is the full source mark. `N` means
NORMAL, `D` means DEMAND. All shown field and mark exclusions are empty and
all shown protection flags are false. Field IDs are f=0, g=4, h=8, Element=11;
the full mark T has local ID1. The wrapper temporary `v` is local var(30).

## Observation scope

The JSON contains native `RunnerPort.onProcess` operands. In
`BlockRunState.process`, the callback runs **before** transfer at that node.
These are dequeued `EdgeDelta` values, not literal post-statement store dumps.
The prepared README's “post-statement” wording is imprecise; this append-only
note corrects that interpretation without editing any frozen/tested input.

“Observed” means the complete current/all-MAY processed JSONL contains the
tuple. “Derived” means the code and the observed input determine it. The
rejected C2 invocation, frozen demand pair and saved-record directory were
not separately dumped. Do not label those three items as captured callbacks.

## Run-1: the demand shape exists in both variants

```java
// Run-1: no previous demand. Observed initial: p.*/F, N (P#1).
Object wrapG(Object p) {
    Object v = getG(p); // Ordinary callee analysis below.
    return v;          // At exit3: observed v.[any]/F and ret.[any]/F, D.
}
// Derived published DATA relation from the observed exit:
// p.*/F -> ret.[any]/F, D.

// Run-1: no previous demand. Observed initial: p.*/F, N (P#1).
Object getG(Object p) {
    return p.g; // At normal exit2: observed ret.[any]/F, D.
}
// Derived published DATA relation from the observed exit:
// p.*/F -> ret.[any]/F, D.
```

The actual exit tuples are in both traces. The native handoff takes the
piece as next entry and the original as next exit:

```text
Observed R1 getG exit:  p.*/F -> ret.[any]/F, D
HandOff.toBackward:    DemandPattern(guidancePattern(piece),
                                   guidancePattern(original))
Derived R2 getG pair:  Dc = ret.[any]/F; Dp = p.*/F
```

`guidancePattern` preserves these two tails. There is no MUST tail or mark
exclusion to normalize in this pair. This is a source-derived handoff pair,
not a direct read of the demand-store object. It is enough to identify the
relevant entry cell; Dp does not participate in initial admission.

## Run-2 current: MAY reaches the inner getter

```java
// Demand from run-1: ret.[any]/F -> p.*/F (derived).
// Root's offered y.[any]/T,D is observed; reversed binding maps it to ret.
// Observed wrapper initial: ret.[any]/F, D (P#2).
Object wrapG(Object p) {
    Object v = getG(p);
    // Backward next: at call1, observed v.[any]/F,D under P#2.
    // Derived reversed BIND_BACK: v.* -> callee.ret.*.
    // Derived inner offer: ret.[any]/F,D.

    return v;
    // Backward first: observed ret.[any]/F,D at return2.
    // The reversed return copy creates the v.[any]/F,D above.
}
// Observed entry0 conclusion after the child returns: p.g.[any]/F,D.
// Derived stored summary: ret.[any]/F -> p.g.[any]/F,D.

// Demand from run-1: ret.[any]/F -> p.*/F (derived).
// Derived offer: ret.[any]/F,D. Observed initial: ret.[any]/F,D (P#2).
Object getG(Object p) {
    return p.g;
    // Backward read: at entry0, observed p.g.[any]/F,D.
    // g fits limit1. MAY is inherited from the initial, not a new cut.
}
// Derived stored summary from observed entry0:
// ret.[any]/F -> p.g.[any]/F,D.
```

The wrapper's observed call operand is `var(30).ANY/F,D`. Its forward
bind-back edge is `ret.* -> var(30).*`. `CallStage.Edges.reversed` reverses
that edge to `var(30).* -> ret.*`; its same-path STAR copy preserves the
actual ANY tail and D support. This is the derived inner offered fact.

## Run-2 all-MAY conversion: first missing required entry

```java
// Same derived demand: ret.[any]/F -> p.*/F.
// Same root offered y.[any]/T,D. The selected conversion changes the seed.
// Observed wrapper initial: ret.*/F,N (P#2).
Object wrapG(Object p) {
    Object v = getG(p);
    // Backward next: at call1, observed v.*/F,N under P#2.
    // Derived reversed BIND_BACK: v.* -> callee.ret.*.
    // Derived inner offer: ret.*/F,N.
    // Current scalar policy: ret.[any]/F demand + ret.*/F offer -> NONE.

    return v;
    // Backward first: observed ret.*/F,N at return2.
}
// No observed backward p.g result. No getG row exists in complete run-2 trace.

// Same derived demand: ret.[any]/F -> p.*/F.
// Derived offer: ret.*/F,N. Emitted initial: NONE (source evaluation).
Object getG(Object p) {
    return p.g; // Body not reached in run-2; zero observed getG rows.
}
// No run-2 getG DATA relation observed.
```

The exact first blocked tuple is:

```text
caller owner:         Nested wrapG1 GWrapper0 [context]
physical call:        instruction1; single root scope, no block decomposition
caller original P:    ret.*/FLOW, initial ID2
observed call operand local(30).*/FLOW, NORMAL
derived child owner:  Nested wrapG1 getG [context]
derived bound offer:  ret.*/FLOW, NORMAL
derived relevant Dc:  ret.[any]/FLOW
scalar result:        null
```

In `RestrictedInitialPolicy.emit`, q=p, demand tail ANY and actual tail STAR
returns null. The candidate changes only private `result` outputs, so it cannot
convert an absent result into a STAR result. `RunMethodCallServices` performs
this admission before installing the current subscription and starting the
callee. Saved replay is earlier; it does not rescue this actual execution.
The complete observed trace shows the inner getter never processes a row.
There is no claim that its complete saved-record directory was captured.

## Run-3 consequence

```java
// Current run-3: exact source output at root0 is observed.
void main() {
    Object s = sourceExactT(); // observed s.$/T,N
    temp.g = s;
    next.f = temp;            // limit2 now retains next.f.g.$/T,N
    Object x = getF(next);    // observed x.g.$/T,N
    Object y = wrapG(x);      // observed y.$/T,N
    out.Element.h = y;        // observed out.Element.h.$/T,N
    sinkExactT(out.Element.h);
}
// Current result: one NORMAL report; run-3 COMPLETE/NO_DEMAND_EDGE.

// All-MAY run-3: the complete trace has only seven ZERO rows at root0..6.
// No source DATA output, wrapper DATA entry or getter DATA result is observed.
// Result: zero NORMAL reports; run-3 COMPLETE/NO_DEMAND_EDGE.
```

The current run-2 marks reach root's source location; the all-MAY backward
route stops earlier. This explains examining source permissions. The actual
sourceHits/seed-index store was not captured; do not claim its literal absence
was dumped. The next forward trace and report difference themselves are
complete native observations.

## Evidence

`wrapG1-first-loss.json` contains thirteen exact event objects with original
input-file line numbers and hashes, complete-case result metadata, source
pins, the derived binding/handoff/policy steps and explicit absence scopes.
`decode-wrapG1-first-loss.py` creates it from the terminal inputs and refuses
to overwrite existing evidence. It launches no analyzer, compiler or test.

The boundary question is now concrete: what should the new policy do with
`ret.[any]/FLOW` demand and `ret.*/FLOW,N` submitted by an ordinary wrapper?
Changing that STAR-actual admission is additional behavior. The tested
MAY-output replacement does not specify it. The user must select the intended
policy before an implementation adds that admission silently.

